    <h1>Codex vs Claude Code vs Gemini CLI: which AI work tool should operators test first?</h1>
    <p>
      This first TripleBench report starts with a practical question: if an operator wants to turn an idea into a live artifact, which AI tool gives the clearest path from instruction to verified output?
    </p>
    <p>
      The current version is a launch scaffold. It defines the structure, score table, and acceptance standard. The final version should replace seed scores with repeated live runs and link to raw prompts.
    </p>

    <h2>Short answer</h2>
    <p>
      Codex is the strongest first test when the work happens inside a local repository and the output must be verified. Claude Code is strong for long agentic sessions and handoff continuity. Gemini CLI is worth testing for research-heavy tasks where broad context and speed matter more than local implementation polish.
    </p>

    <h2>Test task</h2>
    <p>
      Build and ship a small static benchmark site from an empty repository, preserve state in a project file, and leave a handoff prompt for the next agent.
    </p>

    <h2>What to verify before publishing final scores</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Each tool runs the same repository setup task from a clean checkout.</li>
      <li>The site builds locally without warnings that affect output.</li>
      <li>The generated handoff file lets another agent continue without reading the chat transcript.</li>
      <li>Any required human login, payment, or 2FA step is separated from automated progress.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Commercial angle</h2>
    <p>
      The paid product can package the raw prompt set, benchmark CSV, scoring spreadsheet, and operator notes. The free article should answer the search intent; the paid bundle should save readers time if they want to repeat the test.
    </p>
